<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(d) </span><span id="A4.F22.sf4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen3-32B</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 22: </span>Per-layer accuracy (red), content effect (blue), and success rate (green) of zero-shot Qwen-3 models on the logical validity classification task after adding the task difference steering vector <math id="A4.F22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{V-P}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{V-P}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> multiplied by a scalar value <math id="A4.F22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1.5</annotation></semantics></math> at different layers. For comparison, original accuracy and content effect are shown as dashed lines. A success rate lower than 1.0 indicates that after the intervention the model did not produce a label for logical validity classification, i.e., <math id="A4.F22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}\notin\{\mathrm{valid},\mathrm{invalid}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>valid</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>invalid</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}\notin\{\mathrm{valid},\mathrm{invalid}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The orange line indicates the layer that best retains or improves the original accuracy, while lowering content effect and preserving success rate.</figcaption>
</figure>
